# Target C — two-half-disk interface is sublinear under full local labeling

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This note closes one bookkeeping gap in the two-half-disk branch.  The previously
defined group-interface currency counted every strict-rhombus edge on the boundary of
one dominant group whose other side was outside that group.  A thin residual strip was
left as a possible linear source of such edges.  Section 129 locality removes that
possibility for local quadrilateral faces.

The implication chain is

```text
§129: only o(D) strict rhombi are nonlocal
  + adjacent local rhombi have the same harmonic-cell label
  -> every dominant-group interface edge touches a nonlocal rhombus
  -> I_D <= 4(R_4 - sum_r R_r) = o(D)
  -> t + 2 f/D >= 2 + gamma in the two-half-disk branch.
```

The last inequality is weaker than the existing two-half-disk train floor, so this
does not close C-II.  It does remove the residual-interface quantity as an independent
linear obstruction under the stated harmonic-cell labeling.

## 1. Definitions

Use the audited all-regime two-half-disk alternative.  Let `G_1,G_2` be the two
dominant groups of harmonic cells, and let `R_i` be **all** physical strict-rhombus
faces that are local to a cell in `G_i`.  Thus a group includes every local
quadrilateral of each of its cells, not only the quadratic-area subfamily used in the
train extraction.

Let `R_4` be the full strict-rhombus complex and `R_loc` the union of all local
quadrilaterals over all harmonic cells.  Section 129 gives the finite missing-rhombus
bound

\[
N_{\rm miss}:=|R_4\setminus R_{\rm loc}|
 =R_4-\sum_rR_r\le 3c_I+5=o(D).
\tag{1.1}
\]

Let `I_D` count occurrences in the physical boundary of `R_1` or `R_2` whose other
side is occupied by a strict rhombus outside the same dominant group.  Occurrences,
not distinct geometric edges, are counted.

## 2. Local adjacency cannot change the harmonic-cell label

Take two local quadrilateral rhombi sharing a full contact edge.

* If the shared edge has an unloaded endpoint, that endpoint belongs to both local
  harmonic-cell graphs.  The two graphs therefore have the same connected unloaded
  component and the same cell label.
* If both endpoints are loaded, the edge is loaded-loaded.  In the Type-II
  localization this is a force-hull side.  The force hull lies on only one side of a
  hull side, whereas two bounded rhombus faces sharing the edge would occupy opposite
  sides.  Hence this case cannot occur for two bounded local rhombi.

Therefore two adjacent local rhombi always have the same harmonic-cell label.  This is
the same label-persistence step used in the audited groupwise atomic-train reduction;
no contact-isolated source point is removed.

## 3. Interface bound

Consider an occurrence counted by `I_D`.  Its dominant-side rhombus is local by the
definition of `R_i`.  If the opposite rhombus were also local, the label-persistence
claim would put it in the same harmonic cell and hence in the same dominant group,
contrary to the definition of `I_D`.  Thus the opposite strict rhombus is in
`R_4\setminus R_loc`.

Each missing rhombus has four sides and can account for at most one group-boundary
occurrence per side.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{I_D\le 4N_{\rm miss}\le 12c_I+20=o(D).}
\tag{3.1}
\]

This remains valid if one missing rhombus borders two dominant groups on different
sides; the four-side occurrence count already includes that possibility.

## 4. Source-level consequence

The two-half-disk physical-interface transport calculation gives

\[
{I_D\over D}\ge
\left[4+2\gamma-2{q-k\over D}-4{f\over D}-o(1)\right]_+,
\qquad
\gamma={\pi\over8}(4-2\sqrt3).
\tag{4.1}
\]

Insert `I_D=o(D)`.  The positive part can then be removed in the equivalent form,

\[
\boxed{
{q-k\over D}+2{f\over D}
\ge 2+\gamma-o(1).
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

This is a genuine source-level inequality, but it is not a closure by itself.

## 5. Scalar audit against the current train floor

Write

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad \kappa={k\over D},\qquad
C=\liminf {T-q/2\over D},
\]

and use the exact ledger `2f-k=T-q`.  Along a subsequence on which the limits exist,

\[
{f\over D}= {C\over2}-{t\over4}+{\kappa\over4}+o(1),
\qquad
t+2{f\over D}=C+{t\over2}+{\kappa\over2}+o(1).
\tag{5.1}
\]

The two-half-disk atomic-train floor is

\[
C\ge2\sqrt{2\Delta_2}=2.6935473741\ldots,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\tag{5.2}
\]

and the corresponding Section-146 elimination gives `t>=1.9948294847...`.
At the simultaneous endpoint `kappa=0`, `C=2.6935473741...`, and
`t=1.9948294847...`, equation (5.1) gives

\[
{f\over D}=0.8480663159\ldots,
\qquad
t+2f/D=3.6909621166\ldots>2+\gamma
 =2.2104468036\ldots .
\]

Thus (4.2) is already satisfied with substantial slack by the surviving scalar
endpoint.  The interface estimate cannot supply the missing outer-detour or
source-preserving descent theorem.

## 6. Scope and disposition

- The argument uses the audited all-regime two-half-disk alternative, Section 129
  full local-rhombus fidelity, and Type-II loaded-hull localization.
- `I_D=o(D)` is conditional on taking each dominant group to contain all local
  quadrilaterals of its constituent harmonic cells.  If a future grouping uses a
  truncated subcomplex, (3.1) does not apply without restoring the omitted local
  faces.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source.
- No equality uniqueness, global optimality, or finite diameter descent is inferred.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only.  C-II remains
open.  The remaining full-core target is still the aggregate outer-walk detour coupled
to `e_BB-k`, while the macroscopic inactive-container branch is separate.
